On Dec 21, 8:42 pm, Ray Dillinger <b...@sonic.net> wrote:
> James wrote:
> > In the end, if you're writing C, you define a struct, and a
> > lot of functions to manipulate it, and cross your fingers
> > that no one touches it otherwise.

> Well, no.  If I'm working in C and want a struct type to be
> opaque (ie, code in other files can't directly mess with it)
> I let other code use pointers to the struct type, but not the
> type itself.  To express this using C's standard scoping
> rules, I put

> /* actortype - the type of anything in the game that can be used
>    and specialized with triggers or methods in one of the principal
>    roles in an actiontype - includes all monsters, most items,
>    traps, etc. */
> #typedef struct actorstruct * actortype

> in actor.h, where everybody can see it, along with the function
> prototypes, and then

> struct actorstruct {
>    loctype location;
>    spectype species; /* or kind, for inanimate actors*/
>    attrlist attributes;
>    int maxhealth; /* also used as structural integrity for
>                      inanimate actors */
>    int currentdamage;
>    int size;  /* exponential scale, human =3D=3D 0, mass doubles
>                  every 10 points of size, linear dimension
>                  every 30. rings(-180) are the smallest actors. */
>    float speed;
>    /* more stuff here if the game requires it and it can't or
>       shouldn't be managed as an attribute */
> };

> in actor.c along with the functions.  Everybody can see
> actortype and use it, but only the functions in the same file
> with struct actorstruct can see into its structure and
> manipulate its fields.

And you're stuck allocating even the most trivial types
dynamically, and the client code having to manage memory.  It's
a frequent idiom, but it has some serious drawbacks.

